ht give southerners  something  of an advantage during debate because  northerners sometimes we're more likely  to back down rather than inflame a  southerner and I want to offer you and  it's actually from that same thing that  I just found two days ago but as long as  I have it I'm going to just quote you an  example of an inflamed southerner in a  violent incident in this one that  resulted in black eyes apparently one of  them they're both from Mississippi and  one of them said there are no  secessionist in Mississippi and the  other one said yes there are and the  first one said are you saying I'm lying  and the second one said well I suppose  that is what I just said isn't it and  then the first one slugged him and then  there was a fight so this is how the  newspaper reports what happened  Wilcox who's one of them jumped from his  desk and cried I can whip him let me go  waving his arms in the air cries of  where's the sergeant at arms stop the  fighting my god as Wilkins got a knife  no no I don't think he has one a hundred  voices joined in the confusion calling  for the sergeant at arms  the officers etc several of the runner  boys the pages were knocked over in the  excitement and the greatest alarm  prevailed and the account closes with  this very literal description of the  speaker trying to get control of the  house and I'll read it only because I  can't quite believe how literally  descriptive it is the speaker trying to  get control knocked is what it says  basically uses the word knocked saying  in the intervals the chair knock well  here knock no proposition until order  knock knock knock is restored so it  gives you what I love is it and this is  not the only account that I found in  which they people the reporter or the  diarist  actually records what it sounded like  that's an influence southerners that's  kind of typical for what a congressional  fight might be like but that one is  clearly somewhat spontaneous some  violence was not spontaneous some of it  was actually quite deliberate and  strategic and here we come to one  central reason for congressional  violence because when important issues  were at stake fighting men largely  southerners pushed their advantage they  threatened and screamed and insulted and  bullied to silence non-combatants or to  steer their votes  so in essence given the regional  mix of men in Congress bullying and  fighting were politically useful they  were a way of claiming power on the  floor and men who deliberately deployed  this strategy of debate were known and  again this is the 19th century term for  it they were known as bullies you see a  lot of mention in newspapers of bullies  now I actually know nothing about hockey  but whenever I talk about congressional  bullies to anyone who knows anything  about hockey as I've now learned there  are people in hockey called hockey  enforcers and the enforcers their job is  to intimidate people on the opposing  team with threats and violence so  bullies are the congressional equivalent  of hockey enforcers which is a sentence  that I never thought would come out of  my mouth but is the truth and I want to  offer you just one or two examples of  bullying so you can see what I'm talking  about here the first one took place in  1842 when Thomas Arnold a Tennessee Whig  objected to the fact that Southern  Democrats kept silencing John Quincy  Adams to Southern Democrats responded by  coming over to Arnold seat and one of  them very visibly wearing a bowie knife  and threatened to cut Arnold's throat  quote from ear to ear if he ever  objected to a Democrat ever again that's  clear bullying example number two shows  the overall impact of this kind of  bullying and it takes place in 1837 when  freshman representative Joshua Giddings  in Ohio Whig discovered that another  congressman was unfairly claiming a  government per diem and he wanted to  object but his northern friends told him  he'd better shut up and not say anything  because the congressman collecting the  per diem was a duelist and getting's  Micah killed if he piped up and objected  so here you really see the underlying  often invisible impact of congressional  bullying you see men who are too afraid  to speak trying to silence someone else  from speaking up now there are patterns  in this kind of bullying in the 1830s  and much of the 1840s fights tended to  be largely about party  conflict both parties the Whigs and the  Democrats had their share of bullies who  did their best to promote their party  and intimidate the other party and in  this way national parties were kind of  useful because they spread around the  power of southern bullying and gave  northerners and southerners a good  reason to appease each other and  compromise with each other so at first  bullying and violence were party  oriented later in the 1850s they became  more sectional and more centered around  slavery and I'm going to come back to  that idea in just a few minutes so the  answer to question number two why was  there so much violence is largely  because it was useful it actually  accomplished something for at least some  people which leads very naturally to  question number three why didn't someone  stop the violence now one obvious answer  to that question relates to what I just  said which is well some people thought  it was highly useful so they wanted it  to continue but there were other reasons  why the violence persisted a very big  one being that it was very hard to stand  up and object to it without seeming  cowardly and you can see this by looking  at what congressman said when they did  stand up and object to the violence  every now and then and this would  particularly be the case if there was a  mass brawl or a rumble with dozens of  congressmen something really sort of  outrageous someone would stand up  usually a noncombatant and bemoan the  state of congress in speeches that  basically always said what's happening  to congress what is happening to the  state of the nation this violence is  shameful we must do something these  speeches were common enough that John  Quincy Adams actually made up his own  name for them in his diary he refers to  them as lamentation speeches so he'll  describe it'll say in his diary  mr. Smith hit mr. Jones over the head  and then the lamentation speeches  started so the same sorts of speeches  but what's interesting about them is  usually they would have two parts the  first part would be the what's happening  to Congress this is shameful something  should be done what is with the violence  this violence is shameful the second  part would be but of course I'm not  afraid of the violence I'm not afraid of  anyone I'm willing to fight everyone I'm  not scared I'm just speaking generally  about the institution of Congress there  was always chest beating involved after  the sort of objectia  to the congressional violence no one  wanted to seem cowardly this same kind  of logic convinced virtually every  congressman embroiled in a fight to  surrender his congressional privilege of  debate his right not to be held  personally accountable for what he said  on the floor and that's pretty much a  fundamental foundation a free and fair  legislative debate that you will not be  held personally accountable for what you  say during debate but most congressmen  when embroiled in a tangle would declare  that they were willingly surrendering  their privilege of debate because it  would be cowardly to hide behind it so  one of the reasons why the violence  persisted is because it was hard to  object to it without seeming cowardly in  addition Congress did its best to  regulate the violence it didn't try to  stop it  which I think given how violent the  times were and given that some people  thought it was useful would not have  worked anyway  but when it came to fighting Congress  did have an interest in keeping it fair  and keeping Congress functional and it's  kind of counterintuitive but in fact  congressional violence reveals Congress  acting like a community helping to forge  public apologies enforcing unwritten  rules of fair fighting basically keeping  things fair and there's a great example  of this from the 1850s and there's an  account from a congressman who describes  sitting at his desk and he glasses up  and he sees standing across the desk  from one of his colleagues at stranger  glowering with his fists clenched right  and so this fellow who sees this  immediately thinks oh well there's gonna  be a fight there and as he describes his  actions he says he looked at his  colleague and he looked at the stranger  and he thought well my colleague is a  bigger guy so he could probably lick him  in a fight and he went back to work it  was gonna be a fight but my friend will  win few minutes later he looks up and  sure enough the fight has begun and  they're fighting but when he noticed  what he thought might be a gun in the  jacket of the stranger he ran over and  positioned himself behind the stranger  didn't stop the fight just ready to grab  his arm if he reached for the weapon  because an armed man fighting an unarmed  man would be unfair but the fight itself  was fair and so  was going to let it continue and that's  not the only instance of that in which  and as a matter of fact famously that  happens during the caning of Sumner as  well in which someone says stand back  it's a fair fight that happens a lot in  fights as long as that seems fair a fair  fight by the sort of bluntest most  primitive of terms people would let  these men fight and sort of clear their  name so violence was useful violence was  strategic and often it was seen as fair  but as I suggested before it did seem to  give some an advantage to southerners so  what happened when national political  parties combining northerners and  southerners into one team essentially  collapsed in the 1850s and this brings  us to question number four what was the  impact of this violence and what does it  suggest and the answer to this question  does lie in the 1850s in the decade  leading up to the Civil War  now I'll confess to you that as a  historian I usually resist prevailing  assumptions about anything until I see  primary evidence from the period that  I'm studying that proves that it's true  and in my first book which was about  essentially what it felt like to be a  politician on the national stage in the  first decade of our government under the  Constitution  I refuse to assume that there was a  first party system until I saw evidence  that there was one and I never saw that  evidence  I saw temporary alliances I never saw  party discipline I didn't see real  parties I certainly didn't see a system  so the equivalent in the case of this  book is there's a prevailing assumption  that national politics was sort of  swimming along in the first half of the  nineteenth century and that there were  crises and compromises and crises and  compromises until the 1850s when things  suddenly got worse and then we had a  civil war well although I started out  the project refusing to assume that this  was true in fact congressional violence  did get much worse in the 1850s  although national politics was not  necessarily swimming along happily  before that point part of what I'm  showing in my book is that the Civil War  had some very long roots but things did  suddenly get much worse in the 1850s but  why what does this suggest about the  impact of congressional violence  well generally speaking things got more  violent in that decade for a few reasons  for one thing as I've already suggested  national political parties began to  collapse so southerners and northerners  weren't working in league as much as  before and there were thus fewer  restraints on fighting men national  parties can be handy organizational  things that encourage compromise even in  the case of antebellum America sectional  compromise and here they were collapsing  also in the 1850s things got more  violent because at the same time that  national parties were collapsing  sectional parties were rising like the  Republican Party which was essentially a  northern party and the sectional issue  of slavery was coming to the fore as  well and in addition in the 1850s there  was stronger and more organized  anti-slavery sentiment in the north and  more anti-slavery sense congressmen in  Congress and this contributed to  congressional violence as well but in  addition the press played a role in  fueling congressional violence in the  1850s because the press changed in this  period beginning in the mid to late  1840s during this general period of time  there were big technological changes  first you get the steam-powered printing  press then you get the railroads then  you get the Telegraph and when you put  these things together they helped the  press become far more national and reach  and far faster in its ability to spread  news around than before so news could  spread faster and further the audience  watching congressional proceedings was  broader and more national than ever  before people in different parts of the  country could see what other parts of  the country were saying in their  newspapers faster than ever before they  were out of town reporters sitting in  the visitors gallery in the Capitol  building writing columns about Congress  that were communicated throughout the  country pretty quickly more than ever  before and congressmen didn't know often  even who these out-of-town reporters  were so they certainly didn't have  control anymore of what was being said  about Congress so the press is becoming  far more nationalized new  whose is becoming former nationalized  spreading further and faster than before  and these changes really stunned  congressmen they changed the immediate  local and national impact of what  congressman do in Congress they changed  how constituents interacted with their  congressman so in essence these  technological changes in the press  fundamentally change the nature of  congressional politics and you can see  this in an incident that took place in  1850 and actually it's the response to  the incident that shows this most  dramatically but I want to describe at  least what leads up to it and it's in  the Senate and it involves Thomas Hart  Benton of Missouri who's trying to forge  a national compromise on slavery and  Henry foot of Mississippi who's holding  firm on slavery and does not want that  kind of a compromise and Foote is  interested in defeating and discrediting  whatever Benton is saying and doing to  defeat and discredit his attempt at  compromise to do that Foote decides he's  gonna prove that Benton is not a real  southern man he's a traitor to the south  so he shouldn't be trusted his  compromise shouldn't be trusted and to  prove that Benton wasn't a real  southerner Foote began to bully him  insulting him and then essentially  saying things like are you willing to  duel me for that insult because you  should be and if you're not you're not a  real southern man he did this for days  and days and days in Benton actually  really tried to take it and eventually  Benton just snapped  quite literally threw back his chair  almost knocked over his desk lunged at  foot and foot who I think knew sooner or  later this was not going to end well had  armed himself in preparation for this  moment and pulled his gun and aimed it  at Benton which resulted in what one  senator called a stampede with  congressmen running over their desks  grabbing it both congressmen to hold  them back  trying to get their hands on the gun and  standing on chairs to see what was  happening and inevitably when fights  were happening particularly in the house  which was so large there were always  people standing on chairs on the floor  trying to see what was going on in the  committee reports reporting these fights  there's always people who say and then I  jumped up on my chair I couldn't see  what was happening in the  corner of the room now eventually things  calm down people took their seats and  here comes the moment where the press  and the changes in the press and its  implications come through people take  their seats and a New Hampshire senator  stands up and basically says excuse me  for interrupting here gentlemen but  before we begin again with whatever we  were talking about I feel the need to  say that thanks to the Telegraph within  an hour people all over America are  going to be reading that we're  slaughtering each other here in the  Senate and there's absolutely nothing  that we can do about that and you could  virtually hear the stunned silence  because he's right right that greets  that kind of a statement oh yeah we  actually have no control over the spread  of this news and but sure enough it's  gonna spread within an hour  technological changes through these  congressmen off balance news from the  National Center could reach people all  over the nation in a very short amount  of time and one of my favorite this is  my my offering of congressional trivia  for you to take away I am offering you a  gift of congressional trivia that will  be handy at cocktail parties in the  future it's it's relates to this idea  about people suddenly realizing that  they can communicate so much more  quickly with their constituents is so  much more directly and this involves the  word bunk which means claptrap and  nonsense the way we now use it that word  actually originated in Congress which  maybe isn't surprising I don't know but  the the source of it is really  interesting throughout this period  congressmen increasingly realized that  they could stand up and make speeches  aimed back home and not for each other  and early on in this discovery process  there was a congressman from North  Carolina who did this so frequently and  who found I think others found him to be  so tedious with it the frequency with  which he would stand up and say  something that was meant to be  inflammatory but not meant to be heard  on the floor that he would stand up and  say okay folks this is a speech for  Buncombe North Carolina and the other  congressmen would know okay we don't  really have to pay attention these  speeches came to be known as bunk home  speeches and you'll see in people's  letters you know well today was bunk  home day you know everyone stood up and  talked to their constituents and not to  each other  that word essentially gets shortened  down to bunk and that's where that word  comes from so I offer you that piece of  trivia to take away my little gift  you so congressional events are making  their way out to the nation including  Buncombe North Carolina faster than ever  before  and the reverse holds true as well  thanks to that same technology  congressmen know what their constituents  are thinking far more readily than  before and things that congressmen say  back home however extreme were winging  their way to the national center much  more quickly and easily than in the past  so for example in the 1850s  an anti-slavery congressman made a  really flaming anti-slavery speech back  home in Massachusetts and when he got  back to Washington and went back to  Congress a southerner rose during debate  and called him on it basically saying I  know what you said about the South back  in Massachusetts I've read it in the  paper I know that you called us savages  and barbarians do you really think you  can do that and then come back here and  take southerners by the hand and work  with us as colleagues and clearly this  southerner is saying the answer is no so  you can see how this sort of  technological changes are really  complicating congressional proceedings  which were and are fundamentally  grounded on community and compromise  Congress was sectionally polarized  national politics was sexually polarized  the press increasingly was becoming  sectionally polarized and politicians  hadn't yet figured out how to handle  this sort of newfangled press and you  can really see the impact of this state  of affairs in the north in this period  because not only were northerners  electing more anti-slavery congressmen  and more aggressively anti-slavery  congressmen but thanks to the  combination of polarized politics and a  more nationalized press northerners  began actively cheering on their  congressmen to fight in ways that they  never had before they had a better view  of what was going on in Congress they  could see their representative standing  up for their cause and they could see  more of the fighting because more of the  fighting was being reported they liked  what they saw and they told their  representatives that they liked it and  I've seen dozens of these letters from  constituents to their congressmen  basically saying in one way or another  fight them attack them at last there is  a north basically cheering on  northerners to fight southerners in  Congress and meeting it quite literally  most remarkably of all to me in the late  1850s some northerners were sending guns  to their congressmen telling them to  wear them on the floor so they'd show by  the late 1850s you had armed groups of  northerners and southerners running at  each other in the house and you had  reporters commenting that these brawls  looked remarkably like battles which  indeed they were in some ways you could  argue that the first battles of the  Civil War were fought on the floor of  Congress and the press was part of the  tangle of things that contributed to  this state of affairs now of course a  free press is also the very foundation  of a democratic politics and that's an  idea  certainly within our government that  goes back to even before the government  existed it goes all the way back among  other things to James Madison who said  very wisely right at the outset that  given how geographically extended this  Republic was given how large this nation  was the press basically had to be almost  a branch of government because it would  be the only way to enforce  accountability in this new government  the only way to connect people to each  other and their to the government and  vice versa  so the press is an essential part of  democracy so it makes sense that  dramatic changes in the press can  complicate a democratic politics so what  I'm talking about here is dramatic  technological innovations changing the  nature of the press making it more  responsive to the public making  congressmen more immediately accountable  making politics even more polarized and  changing the nature of Congress that's  happening in the 1850s and some version  of that is happening now which brings us  back to the present because I think in  the same way that the 19th century  Congress was figuring out how to handle  the telegraph the current Congress is  struggling to figure out how to handle  the internet and cell phones and 24/7  cable news coverage today as in the 19th  century technology has suddenly made the  connection between congressmen and their  constituents more immediate more direct  and Congress  and the public haven't quite figured out  what this means or how to handle the new  vulnerabilities and capabilities being  caused by it now obviously this is the  evocative and I think interesting  parallel that I mentioned at the  beginning of my talk  oddly enough the same anxiety and  confusion and grandstanding and  extremism that my nineteenth-century  congressmen were grappling with are in  the air again now as I said at the  outset I'm not offering answers here I'm  not offering advice here  equally important I'm definitely not  comparing the present day to the lead-up  to the Civil War there are vast  differences of politics of policies and  of party dynamics that make it  impossible to line up these two periods  as equivalent but what I am saying is  that technology plays an interesting and  I think sometimes underappreciated role  in the polarization of a democratic  politics and certainly it did in the  first half of 19th century and I think  this is a really interesting moment  because I think just in the last I don't  know 10 8 to 10 years we're watching  politics figure out what it can and  can't do with technology as well thank  you very much


